00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:21,110
Right?

55
00:05:21,110 --> 00:05:26,110
So, what are some things that get lost, and you wouldn't even know about it when you do this kind of normalization and flattening? What gets lost?

56
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:27,670
That's a great point, right?

57
00:05:27,670 --> 00:05:36,550
So, usually we put this data, let's say this simple example of customers, products, and and their purchases, orders, transactions, right?

58
00:05:36,550 --> 00:05:41,470
Usually we would put this into a database or into a data warehouse, uh and then we run SQL queries over that.

59
00:05:41,480 --> 00:05:47,790
And SQL queries, they're really good at telling me what happened last week, what happened last month.

60
00:05:47,790 --> 00:05:58,070
Um I can ask all kinds of historical questions, but it's less about that and the prediction is not about understanding the history, it's about understanding the future.

61
00:05:58,070 --> 00:06:02,990
So, what do I have to do, let's say, as a data scientist?

62
00:06:02,990 --> 00:06:08,870
Is I cannot just train a model over these three tables and say, "Just learn over these tables and tell me whether a customer will churn.

63
00:06:08,870 --> 00:06:10,711
What's the risk of that?" Right?

64
00:06:10,711 --> 00:06:22,830
What I have to do is I have to manually, for example, take the customer, take their transaction history, and then somehow I need to aggregate that transaction history into what is called a feature.

65
00:06:22,830 --> 00:06:25,430
I need to basically do the table flattening.

66
00:06:25,430 --> 00:06:28,110
I need to do feature engineering.

67
00:06:28,110 --> 00:06:34,150
I need to summarize your past purchases into some count, into some numbers, so I can say, "How much did you purchase last week?

68
00:06:34,150 --> 00:06:36,430
How much did you purchase last month?"

69
00:06:36,430 --> 00:06:40,351
