70
00:06:40,351 --> 00:06:42,991
How about Wednesdays? How about mornings?

71
00:06:42,991 --> 00:06:45,909
How about afternoons?"

72
00:06:45,909 --> 00:06:47,750
And then it's maybe what's the sum of purchase prices?

73
00:06:47,760 --> 00:06:49,750
What's your median purchase price?

74
00:06:49,760 --> 00:06:53,631
What's the most expensive product you bought? What's the cheapest one?

75
00:06:53,631 --> 00:06:56,270
And all these variables we can use 2 hours here.

76
00:06:56,280 --> 00:07:02,909
Yeah. For me to just go through this, they could be predictive of your likelihood of churn, right?

77
00:07:02,909 --> 00:07:07,550
And now I only connected the user, the customer, with their transaction history.

78
00:07:07,550 --> 00:07:09,790
How about the products?

79
00:07:09,790 --> 00:07:14,231
Right now, if I go to the products, I'm like, "What types of products? What categories?

80
00:07:14,231 --> 00:07:18,909
How well are those products rated? Do they have a high star rating? Do they have a low star rating?

81
00:07:18,909 --> 00:07:20,471
Are those products popular?" And so on, right?

82
00:07:20,471 --> 00:07:29,670
So, it's like this infinite possibility of signals that I, as human or as my coding agent,

83
00:07:29,670 --> 00:07:34,390
could engineer into this big training table, so I could then train the model.

84
00:07:34,390 --> 00:07:46,311
And basically training the model maybe is easy, but where it gets complicated is when this model is running, let's say, in production, when it's making these predictions, all of these signals need to be updated. Right?

85
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:59,270
If I make one more transaction, all my counts of transactions, all my aggregations of transactions, all they need to be updated because everything changed because I added a new transaction.

86
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:01,790
Right?

87
00:08:01,790 --> 00:08:09,190
So, you start seeing how is this getting complicated because then on the fly, the signals need to be updated with every single transaction,

88
00:08:09,190 --> 00:08:18,790
with every single update to the database, all these downstream calculations need to be spun off because everything changes because there's a new piece of information.

89
00:08:18,790 --> 00:08:27,030
And there is other problems with this flattening where, as you hinted, essentially you are losing information, right?

90
00:08:27,030 --> 00:08:32,550
There is more information in, let's say, in the raw sequence than there is in the count over the last 7 days.

91
00:08:32,560 --> 00:08:46,150
Count over last 7 days is some arbitrary construct that we humans are like, "Okay, probably if if I count how much you purchased last week, that will likely tell me something useful about you purchasing in the future as well."

92
00:08:46,160 --> 00:08:47,910
But it's a guess.

93
00:08:47,910 --> 00:08:54,310
So, I have to first engineer the feature, put it in the model, retrain the model, and then see, "Oh, did it help or not?"

94
00:08:54,310 --> 00:08:56,552
Yes, it helped a bit. Okay, fine. All right?

95
00:08:56,552 --> 00:09:00,590
And then, if you, for example, start thinking broader, right?

96
00:09:00,590 --> 00:09:02,350
If you think about let's say, fraud detection.

97
00:09:02,350 --> 00:09:04,829
Fraud detection is an adversarial game.

98
00:09:04,839 --> 00:09:12,990
So, whatever model you are using to predict fraud, the other side figures that out and tries to game you.

99
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:17,230
Because it games you, your model accuracy starts decreasing.

100
00:09:17,230 --> 00:09:23,350
And then you are like, "Okay, what signal am I missing to detect these new types of fraud?"

101
00:09:23,350 --> 00:09:24,910
And you put it in, they discover it, and you are behind again.

102
00:09:24,910 --> 00:09:31,351
And you are again scratching the head, "What another signal do I need to do to do this?" Right?

103
00:09:31,351 --> 00:09:34,670
So, it's super painful, super manual, super expensive.

104
00:09:34,670 --> 00:09:42,950
And studies show that most of these models never see production because it's so old school. It's like 30 years old technology.

105
00:09:42,950 --> 00:09:45,350
We've been doing this the same way for 30 years.

106
00:09:45,350 --> 00:09:51,190
Maybe we changed the architecture that we train on this data, but the architecture is not the problem.

107
00:09:51,190 --> 00:09:57,550
The problem is this manual process of flattening the tables, engineering the features, training these models,

108
00:09:57,560 --> 00:10:04,550
putting these models in production by needing to update all these features in real time as events are happening.

109
00:10:04,550 --> 00:10:14,430
And then you have problems with what information leakage or this time correctness because you know, maybe I have a mistake and I also count the transaction you'll make tomorrow.

110
00:10:14,430 --> 00:10:25,030
And then of course, from data about tomorrow, I can predict tomorrow very accurately, but you don't catch that and then your models don't perform the way I you expected and so on.

111
00:10:25,030 --> 00:10:31,510
So it's a I would say data science and this model building with machine learning is very, very hard, very, very hard to get very wrong on.

112
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:41,350
So in a way, you can take a relational database, you can manually create the views and materialized views and triggers and all that, right?

113
00:10:41,350 --> 00:10:47,590
To make it look like a graph, but really the devil's in the details because doing that is pretty complicated and a lot of work, right?

114
00:10:47,590 --> 00:10:56,510
It sounds like your thesis is if you can do all that work to take something that's normalized and turn that into a graph after all this burning of energy, why not start with a graph?

115
00:10:56,520 --> 00:10:58,030
Exactly.

116
00:10:58,030 --> 00:10:59,790
So my thesis is the following, right?

117
00:10:59,790 --> 00:11:05,790
The first thing is that tabular data, this relational data, is the key to enterprise decision making.

118
00:11:05,800 --> 00:11:08,110
Mhm. Right?

119
00:11:08,110 --> 00:11:12,110
We collect it, it's the most valuable data because we want to make accurate decisions at all levels, right?

120
00:11:12,120 --> 00:11:20,510
And even in agentic world, agents need to make decisions and those decisions have to be driven in data and historical patterns.

121
00:11:20,510 --> 00:11:22,790
They are not common sense type things most often, right?

122
00:11:22,790 --> 00:11:26,510
So that's the first thing is this is amazingly valuable.

123
00:11:26,510 --> 00:11:31,070
Second thing that I want to say is that today's AI does not understand this type of data well.

124
00:11:31,070 --> 00:11:38,470
You cannot textify a database, put it as a prompt in chat GPT, and expect it to work.

125
00:11:38,470 --> 00:11:40,310
People have tried that, they burned themselves badly.

126
00:11:40,310 --> 00:11:44,870
All right, so it's kind of a missing data modality in today's AI.

127
00:11:44,870 --> 00:11:46,670
So, how do you then solve the problem?

128
00:11:46,670 --> 00:11:54,150
That was the key, let's say, question or insight that I asked myself and my students here at Stanford and said, we have to do something in this area, it's left behind.

129
00:11:54,150 --> 00:12:03,750
So, we developed this approach, we call it a relational deep learning, that basically says, we can take a database, a set of tables,

130
00:12:03,750 --> 00:12:06,870
we can represent it as a graph of linkages,

131
00:12:06,870 --> 00:12:14,990
and now we can develop special architectures that generalize this attention mechanism that is prevalent in text transformers,

132
00:12:14,990 --> 00:12:24,270
where we are attending over the tokens, the words from the past, to now generalize this to attend over the tables in a database.

133
00:12:24,280 --> 00:12:31,470
And if we can have now a neural network that attends over the raw tables in a database,

134
00:12:31,470 --> 00:12:39,590
and just it basically figures out how to pull all these data together into an accurate prediction, there are two benefits.

135
00:12:39,590 --> 00:12:47,590
First benefit is that you get an amazing productivity gain, because you don't have to do this flattening of data into a single table, and you can build these models faster.

136
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:53,110
The second improvement is that your models get kind of superhuman accuracy.

137
00:12:53,120 --> 00:13:05,750
And the reason for that is that, as I mentioned earlier, when you take a sequence of transactions and aggregate it into a transaction count over the last 7 days, you have thrown away a lot of information.

138
00:13:05,750 --> 00:13:18,151
So, attention mechanism that attends actually over a sequence of transactions has much more fidelity, has much more finesse to it, to actually learn how to combine this information, how to attend over these transactions. Is it 7 days?

139
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:20,271
Is it mornings? Is it evenings?

140
00:13:20,271 --> 00:13:27,590
Let the attention mechanism figure that out to combine that data in an optimal way that is most predictive downstream.

141
00:13:27,590 --> 00:13:35,350
And, of course, it's not only because it's a graph, it's multiple tables, it's not just oh, it's a sequence modeling.

142
00:13:35,350 --> 00:13:37,510
No, it's more because it's recursive, right?

143
00:13:37,510 --> 00:13:43,030
A sequence of transactions connects to a sequence of products.

144
00:13:43,030 --> 00:13:48,750
Those products connect to other transactions that connect to other users.

145
00:13:48,750 --> 00:13:55,390
So, now you are attending through this graph essentially to understand and to reason over the data in a database.

146
00:13:55,400 --> 00:14:03,470
That's the exciting thing is that you go from a person to transaction to the product to another transaction to another person.

147
00:14:03,470 --> 00:14:10,430
All of a sudden, you can be like, "Okay, what are the characteristics of people that buy the same products as I buy?" There's useful information there.

148
00:14:10,440 --> 00:14:13,350
Mhm. Right? And so on and so forth. That's the thesis.

149
00:14:13,360 --> 00:14:21,550
That's interesting because if you again going back to classical, I guess, transformers, you're attending over a sequence of tokens in text, right?

150
00:14:21,560 --> 00:14:25,030
What does a token look like in this model?

151
00:14:25,030 --> 00:14:28,870
I mean, what is conceptually we've been thinking about tokens in text as, you know, three, four letters, three, four characters.

152
00:14:28,870 --> 00:14:30,550
There's some kind of arbitrary boundary, right?

153
00:14:30,550 --> 00:14:34,830
What becomes your unit of attending over in this model? Like, what is a token here?

154
00:14:34,839 --> 00:14:36,990
Great question.

155
00:14:36,990 --> 00:14:39,030
A token here, you have two options.

156
00:14:39,030 --> 00:14:41,950
One option is to think of every cell in a database as token.

157
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:44,750
So, every row column combination, right?

158
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:51,070
An age of a particular user, uh location of a particular user, gender of a particular user, and so on, right?

159
00:14:51,070 --> 00:14:53,350
Those could be the tokens.

160
00:14:53,350 --> 00:14:56,390
Or you can think of entire row as a token.

161
00:14:56,390 --> 00:15:03,990
So, a user is a token, a transaction is a token, a product is a token that then gets attended over. And actually, in reality, that's maybe the way to see it.

162
00:15:03,990 --> 00:15:13,829
In reality, it's a bit more complicated, but probably the best way to think is that now we are attending over individual cells and as well as how they point to each other.

163
00:15:13,829 --> 00:15:19,829
That's the key differentiator is that we're not only attending over individual cells, but also over the relationships between the cells, right?

164
00:15:19,839 --> 00:15:22,630
That one row points to another row.

165
00:15:22,630 --> 00:15:34,350
so I know that this particular user points to this particular transaction and that transactions point to a particular product that has a given set of entities.

166
00:15:34,350 --> 00:15:36,510
So, the attention mechanism is different.

167
00:15:36,510 --> 00:15:38,470
It's not sequence based, but it's like graph based.

168
00:15:38,470 --> 00:15:42,910
So, you need to understand these relationships and pointers.

169
00:15:42,910 --> 00:15:45,910
Understanding this relational structure is super crucial,

170
00:15:45,910 --> 00:15:54,270
especially when the data is noisy or in cold start type regimes where you don't have enough information,

171
00:15:54,270 --> 00:16:01,870
then through this relational context, the model is able to hone in and make much more accurate predictions than traditional approaches.

172
00:16:01,880 --> 00:16:04,150
Because you know, I mean, to my ear it keeps getting blown away.

173
00:16:04,150 --> 00:16:08,071
Going I'm going back to our price transformer, right? Simple tokens, simple embeddings.

174
00:16:08,080 --> 00:16:16,190
So, what would then be your embedding model or your tokenizer model because you have a lot more volatility and variation in what a token is, right?

175
00:16:16,190 --> 00:16:19,990
In a simple text like the cats sat on the mat, right?

176
00:16:19,990 --> 00:16:21,910
You have a much more narrow or more limited token space, right?

177
00:16:21,910 --> 00:16:29,150
But here, almost it sounds like almost every combination, every entity, every entity relationship, you potentially have an unbounded token space then, don't you?

178
00:16:29,160 --> 00:16:31,270
Great point.

179
00:16:31,270 --> 00:16:33,230
You have an unbounded token space.

180
00:16:33,230 --> 00:16:39,110
You don't say, "Oh, you know, I have this fixed vocabulary of tokens as you do in LLMs."

181
00:16:39,110 --> 00:16:42,829
Here, you have in this respect an infinite number of tokens, right?

182
00:16:42,829 --> 00:16:46,550
Or token combinations because cells can take arbitrary values. There can be text in there.

183
00:16:46,550 --> 00:16:49,510
There can be images in there.

184
00:16:49,510 --> 00:16:57,230
So, in this respect, you don't have this kind of explicit tokens, but what are you attending over are the values of the cells.

185
00:16:57,230 --> 00:17:03,230
In this respect, you're right, the token space is infinite because I'm attending over the values of individual cells.

186
00:17:03,240 --> 00:17:08,670
Mhm. I mean, technically even in English, you have a fairly large token space. We just approximate it down, I'm guessing.

187
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:10,270
For example, right?

188
00:17:10,270 --> 00:17:14,350
Or at the end we can say, "Okay, we have characters and there's letters of the alphabet." And that's it, right?

189
00:17:14,360 --> 00:17:16,790
Mhm. Everything is made out of that.

190
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:30,030
So, does then the attention mechanism itself have a different meaning because again, going back to relational transformers, you have your n squared attention for every token versus every token very very conceptually and dumbed down, right?

191
00:17:30,030 --> 00:17:35,710
What then here would be the complexity or space complexity of this attention mechanism over this graph kind of tokens?

192
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:37,630
Yeah, it's different, right?

193
00:17:37,630 --> 00:17:43,870
Because you don't think of this as oh, I have now this sequence of tokens and I do everything to everything type attention.

