From a training time perspective, resource perspective, how would you contrast those two?

392
00:35:12,230 --> 00:35:18,071
LLMs on the other hand are pretty heavy on this side, but in the same space, how would you compare? What are the contrasts?

393
00:35:18,080 --> 00:35:20,030
Yeah, it's interesting.

394
00:35:20,030 --> 00:35:26,950
I think today for majority of predictive modeling, it's actually not the training time that's the bottleneck.

395
00:35:26,950 --> 00:35:36,710
It's all these data munging, ETL, data pipelines that are actually running on CPUs and are very slow and sluggish.

396
00:35:36,710 --> 00:35:41,470
And the models that we train on top, those are quite quick to train.

397
00:35:41,470 --> 00:35:53,270
So, the difference here is that we say, "Skip the ETL, take a beefy model, GPU-based, and just let it train directly on the raw data."

398
00:35:53,270 --> 00:35:55,710
And I want to make a point, right?

399
00:35:55,710 --> 00:36:05,671
It's like earlier I made claims, you can now build models faster, you can get 10-20% better accuracy, and people are like, "Why? How?"

400
00:36:05,671 --> 00:36:07,790
And my point is, we shouldn't be surprised about this.

401
00:36:07,800 --> 00:36:12,630
The same steps carried out twice already.

402
00:36:12,630 --> 00:36:17,830
First, they carried out in computer vision, and then they carried out in, let's say, natural language understanding.

403
00:36:17,830 --> 00:36:21,990
And if you look at computer vision, I could say, if I joke a bit, right?

404
00:36:21,990 --> 00:36:26,750
I'm building a detector whether there is a cow on the image or not.

405
00:36:26,750 --> 00:36:35,750
Of course, you could say, "If me, computer vision expert, I can engineer a perfect feature for whether there's a cow on the image or not, then my classifier will be amazing."

406
00:36:35,760 --> 00:36:45,910
We are both smiling now because we both know that it's kind of impossible to human-engineer a perfect feature for is there a cow in the image or not, right?

407
00:36:45,920 --> 00:36:51,230
So, all these feature-based approaches that we would do, I don't know, sift features,

408
00:36:51,230 --> 00:37:02,790
Gabor filters, and so on, where you would then train a neural network or a support vector machine or whatever it was to detect whether there is a cow on the image or not, you don't do this today.

409
00:37:02,790 --> 00:37:04,590
You just have a a vision transformer or a,

410
00:37:04,590 --> 00:37:12,190
you know, in the old days you had a convolutional neural network that just attends over all the pixels and

411
00:37:12,190 --> 00:37:18,550
figures out how to combine all this scattered spread information across the pixels into is it a cow or not.

412
00:37:18,550 --> 00:37:20,750
What I'm doing here or proposing is the same.

413
00:37:20,760 --> 00:37:25,830
Don't try to engineer the perfect feature.

414
00:37:25,830 --> 00:37:32,990
Let the attention mechanism attend over your database the same way as it attends over the pixels and let it extract that information out.

415
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:47,270
So, in this respect, I'm kind of saying very obvious things that we've seen work in the past and maybe the only thing I'm saying is as soon as you think of a database as a graph, you can do it.

416
00:37:47,280 --> 00:37:53,430
Before, we didn't know how to represent the database to be able to kind of attend over it.

417
00:37:53,430 --> 00:37:56,150
We didn't know what the pixels are.

418
00:37:56,150 --> 00:37:58,870
And now I'm saying, "Think of it as a graph.

419
00:37:58,870 --> 00:38:03,431
Nodes are your pixels and there are relationships between them. Just attend over that."

420
00:38:03,431 --> 00:38:11,190
It's more complex than computer vision because there is no this spatial locality and things like that, but it's very doable and very fruitful.

421
00:38:11,200 --> 00:38:17,550
I can go back to I think when you said treat the database as a graph, that I guess triggered something for me in a good way.

422
00:38:17,550 --> 00:38:21,430
When you have a prediction query or prediction request or prediction session coming in, right?

423
00:38:21,440 --> 00:38:26,150
What does the data access pattern look like in your database?

424
00:38:26,150 --> 00:38:29,350
From the audition mechanism, from the model perspective, from the flow perspective.

425
00:38:29,360 --> 00:38:31,550
That's a great point.

426
00:38:31,550 --> 00:38:38,110
So, what the data access mechanism essentially is, as events are coming in, you need to keep the graph up to date.

427
00:38:38,110 --> 00:38:45,590
And then all you need to do is basically fetch like a local neighborhood around the node of interest, right?

428
00:38:45,590 --> 00:38:57,150
So, you need to basically fetch a small subset of your database, data from a couple of tables, send that through the neural network, through this transformer-based architecture, to get a prediction.

429
00:38:57,160 --> 00:39:04,510
And what is a huge difference here is that once the model is trained, putting it in production is trivial.

430
00:39:04,520 --> 00:39:06,910
You just refresh the raw data.

431
00:39:06,910 --> 00:39:15,150
There's no feature pipelines, no feature stores, no that additional computation that needs to be done every time a transaction appears.

432
00:39:15,150 --> 00:39:19,590
You just take the latest data, pump it through the neural network, and you get the latest prediction.

433
00:39:19,600 --> 00:39:31,190
So, how do databases need to adapt and evolve to allow these patterns to be visible to the model or I guess the agent, right?

434
00:39:31,190 --> 00:39:34,030
We can go full graph DBs, right?

435
00:39:34,030 --> 00:39:36,910
Or we can have more specialized indexes on the current graphs, but something's going to break.

436
00:39:36,920 --> 00:39:43,470
That is a great point. So, I would actually say to me there are two solutions here.

437
00:39:43,470 --> 00:39:47,270
If you don't need too much QPS,

438
00:39:47,270 --> 00:39:52,870
then you can basically just keep the data in Postgres or MySQL or in Snowflake or whatever you have it,

439
00:39:52,880 --> 00:40:00,830
and we can basically do this push down to extract the data on the fly through essentially running the SQL statements.

440
00:40:00,840 --> 00:40:10,470
If you need performance, scale, and things like that, and short latency, or if the mass of predictions you need to make is super large,

441
00:40:10,480 --> 00:40:20,230
basically the graph databases, the current graph databases, are not the right approach because they've been built for a different type of workload.

442
00:40:20,240 --> 00:40:26,110
They've been built for this kind of sparkle-like queries and for building the graphs and querying them.

443
00:40:26,110 --> 00:40:29,750
They haven't been built for the AI workloads.

444
00:40:29,760 --> 00:40:40,870
So, what we see is that today's graph databases are about an order two orders of magnitude too slow to actually support this type of predictive AI use cases.

445
00:40:40,880 --> 00:40:43,150
On the read or write path or both paths?

446
00:40:43,160 --> 00:40:45,150
Mostly on the read. Okay.

447
00:40:45,160 --> 00:40:48,510
On just extracting these subgraphs out.

448
00:40:48,520 --> 00:40:55,790
So, what we've built at Kumo is a specialized system built ground up.

449
00:40:55,800 --> 00:41:02,470
You can think of it as a graph database that is purpose-built for these AI workloads for high-volume,

450
00:41:02,470 --> 00:41:11,270
large-scale predictions that can be run and has been adopted at kind of the largest internet-scale enterprises here in Silicon Valley and beyond.

451
00:41:11,280 --> 00:41:13,110
Nice, nice.

452
00:41:13,110 --> 00:41:15,550
I know you have talked about PyTorch Geometric.

453
00:41:15,550 --> 00:41:17,550
I know it's sort of a tangent, but how did it come about?

454
00:41:17,550 --> 00:41:20,391
What was the origin story? Where is it today?

455
00:41:20,400 --> 00:41:27,990
That's a great point. So, PyTorch Geometric basically came out of this idea.

456
00:41:27,990 --> 00:41:29,510
This was back in 2017, 2019, something like that, right?

457
00:41:29,510 --> 00:41:35,870
Like when deep learning was raising up, it was computer vision, transformers were kind of there, but not yet.

458
00:41:35,870 --> 00:41:40,950
And then there was a lot of problems that don't fit into this fixed grid of pixels.

459
00:41:40,950 --> 00:41:45,230
They don't fit into a sequence, but they fit into the graph.

460
00:41:45,230 --> 00:41:53,190
And this is a lot of problems in let's say spatial data, computer graphics, chemistry, social networks, graphs, and so on.

461
00:41:53,190 --> 00:42:04,950
And there was really a need to build an open-source package where researchers could build latest architectures, benchmark them with each other, and for the community to make progress.

462
00:42:04,950 --> 00:42:09,790
So, we built PyG or PyTorch Geometric as the library.

463
00:42:09,790 --> 00:42:13,670
It has, I think, like now 20 plus thousand GitHub stars and things like that.

464
00:42:13,670 --> 00:42:17,550
That really catalyzed the research in this area of graph neural networks and graph transformers.

465
00:42:17,550 --> 00:42:20,470
We built also benchmarks.

466
00:42:20,470 --> 00:42:25,670
One of them was called OGB, Open Graph Benchmark as we called it, large-scale graphs.

467
00:42:25,670 --> 00:42:34,390
And now for relational data, we are building what we call a RelBench, which is a set of databases and a set of predictive tasks over them.

468
00:42:34,390 --> 00:42:41,150
And all are publicly accessible so that we can benchmark progress and see what methods work and which don't.

469
00:42:41,150 --> 00:42:53,110
And I would say for PyG was also great because we have great collaborations with Nvidia as well as Intel in the past, where we said, "Okay, this has to run efficiently, scalably on the latest hardware."

470
00:42:53,110 --> 00:43:04,270
And those partnerships and that support with Nvidia, with the PyTorch team and so on means that the open-source library is actually useful and performant.

471
00:43:04,280 --> 00:43:07,950
You mentioned Nvidia and hardware companies.

472
00:43:07,950 --> 00:43:12,630
Are you finding that there are other kind of demands or expectations that

473
00:43:12,630 --> 00:43:21,510
would serve relational foundational models better from a hardware perspective than let's say what they currently serving today, which is the LLM use case, right?

474
00:43:21,510 --> 00:43:26,150
Or are you making do with what's coming out, the usual H100s and Yeah, and B200s and so on, yeah.

475
00:43:26,150 --> 00:43:28,150
That's a great point.

476
00:43:28,150 --> 00:43:35,670
Working with graphs, I'd say it's an order of magnitude harder than working with these linear data types, right?

477
00:43:35,670 --> 00:43:41,230
Text is nice, it's a sequence, you can chop it, you can just linearly scan through a GPU.

478
00:43:41,230 --> 00:43:43,670
Image is essentially it's a fixed-size matrix.

479
00:43:43,680 --> 00:43:49,270
Each images are independent from each other, so again, you can kind of push them through or video in the same way.

480
00:43:49,280 --> 00:43:51,510
But graphs are hard, right?

481
00:43:51,510 --> 00:43:57,870
Graphs, they have no up and down, they don't have any left and right, there's no way to chop them into pieces.

482
00:43:57,870 --> 00:44:02,070
Everything is kind of connected and interdependent, you don't know what is going to connect with what.

483
00:44:02,080 --> 00:44:07,190
So dealing with graphs is very, very hard.

484
00:44:07,190 --> 00:44:16,150
And this means that you have to be very careful how you design the systems to be scalable and to actually take advantage of today's hardware.

485
00:44:16,150 --> 00:44:19,430
And I would say through Stanford we're actually

486
00:44:19,430 --> 00:44:30,310
working with Nvidia quite a lot in terms of them understanding what are the needs for the future chips as we move beyond sequential LLMs.

487
00:44:30,310 --> 00:44:37,910
And memory access patterns become very different because you are essentially almost like randomly accessing different nodes in memory,

488
00:44:37,920 --> 00:44:42,350
pulling those together, and you want to keep your GPUs utilized.

489
00:44:42,360 --> 00:44:44,750
Mhm. Can you give us a peek at what's coming?

490
00:44:44,760 --> 00:44:53,910
I can say that we've been discussing and brainstorming what would allow us to process these types of interconnected data in a proper way,

491
00:44:53,910 --> 00:44:57,710
and it's a lot about defining the benchmarks and running simulations,

492
00:44:57,710 --> 00:45:07,910
running measurements to understand how can these data access patterns be better supported by the underlying, I would say, software as well as the hardware.

493
00:45:07,920 --> 00:45:10,071
Mhm. Interesting.

494
00:45:10,071 --> 00:45:20,150
And I guess in a way, given that we're talking about not exabytes of data, not the amount of data volume at the same scale as LLMs, there's opportunity for even more specific hardware to come out of this.

495
00:45:20,160 --> 00:45:22,550
Is that fair? It's interesting, right?

496
00:45:22,550 --> 00:45:26,750
I think these enterprise data sets get big very, very quickly.

497
00:45:26,760 --> 00:45:28,910
Mhm. Right?

498
00:45:28,910 --> 00:45:36,910
Maybe they are not internet scale, but they get big very, very quickly if you start thinking about, I don't know, all the transactions on the Bitcoin blockchain, right?

499
00:45:36,910 --> 00:45:42,030
Now, all the clicks and all the comments made by all the users of Reddit.

500
00:45:42,030 --> 00:45:43,630
These amounts of data get very big.

501
00:45:43,630 --> 00:45:50,950
Or if you start thinking about banks, financial institutions, transactions, and everything that's going on there, that's huge velocity and volumes of data.

502
00:45:50,960 --> 00:45:53,350
Easily petabytes and up.

503
00:45:53,360 --> 00:46:04,430
Nice. You know, in the aspect of foundation models, it's when you think LLMs, we think we are all now implicitly aware of our how experience hallucinations, right?

504
00:46:04,430 --> 00:46:06,270
What does hallucination mean in the context of RFMs?

505
00:46:06,280 --> 00:46:07,831
Is it there? It's not there.

506
00:46:07,831 --> 00:46:09,591
It's look differently. What does it mean?

507
00:46:09,600 --> 00:46:11,310
That's a great point.

508
00:46:11,310 --> 00:46:17,150
I think what is different in relation of foundation models because they are making a prediction,

509
00:46:17,150 --> 00:46:29,630
we make sure that that prediction is calibrated so that we properly give the estimate of uncertainty and also get back a sense of accuracy.

510
00:46:29,630 --> 00:46:36,150
So, in this respect, it is not hallucinated, but basically you get an accurate estimate of how sure we how accurate is this prediction.

511
00:46:36,150 --> 00:46:40,830
And then with that, you can then decide what to do.

512
00:46:40,830 --> 00:46:44,910
So, because we can train for prediction and we properly penalize these models, right?

513
00:46:44,910 --> 00:46:49,230
The problem with LLMs in some sense is that they don't understand numbers.

514
00:46:49,230 --> 00:46:53,990
They just say, "Did I generate the next token correctly or not?"

515
00:46:53,990 --> 00:46:56,990
So, if the next token is 200 or 2,000, it's the same amount of penalty.

516
00:46:57,000 --> 00:47:06,150
But in relation of foundation models, if my prediction is 1,000 off, the model gets much more than if the prediction is two or 1.5 off.

517
00:47:06,160 --> 00:47:08,230
How do you do that penalization?

518
00:47:08,230 --> 00:47:11,710
What is the mechanism for actually penalizing a bad prediction?

519
00:47:11,720 --> 00:47:25,150
Yeah, because we have loss function that is actually not about was the next token generated correctly, but we can kind of measure the distance if you want to think of it this way between the truth and the prediction.

520
00:47:25,150 --> 00:47:29,390
And we penalize by the amount of distance, not by was it correct or not.

521
00:47:29,400 --> 00:47:31,150
Right?

522
00:47:31,150 --> 00:47:35,070
And in text, you cannot do that because there's no similarity between tokens.

523
00:47:35,070 --> 00:47:39,151
Tokens are tokens and it's either the correct one or it's the wrong one. There is nothing in between.

524
00:47:39,160 --> 00:47:43,030
In code, which is one of the applications of LLMs, LLMs, right?

525
00:47:43,040 --> 00:47:52,070
You do have some kind of penalization in the whole RL loop where you might penalize it as it compiles, as it do something, does it do some of these things.

526
00:47:52,070 --> 00:47:55,150
You're right, it's not accurate, it's not perfect, but there is some kind of guidance there, right?

527
00:47:55,160 --> 00:48:03,670
In these domains where it's verifiable, of course, RL has shown a really good progress, but even there, the verification signal is binary.

528
00:48:03,670 --> 00:48:06,110
It compiles or it doesn't.

529
00:48:06,110 --> 00:48:11,830
It's not that something is more accurate than something else. It's just yes or no. That's kind of what I'm trying to say.

530
00:48:11,840 --> 00:48:15,590
But in these prediction use cases, you get more information.

531
00:48:15,590 --> 00:48:21,710
You kind of know how much you were wrong, and you can tell that the model, and then the model starts to learn these nuances in a better way.

532
00:48:21,720 --> 00:48:23,750
Nice.

533
00:48:23,750 --> 00:48:27,910
Is it also, again, for layman, is it also kind of a backprop mechanism or is it a different setting that feedback back again?

534
00:48:27,920 --> 00:48:30,870
It's the backprop. It's just the loss functions are different.

535
00:48:30,880 --> 00:48:33,230
Yeah, nice. The penalty functions.

536
00:48:33,240 --> 00:48:35,630
Right.

537
00:48:35,630 --> 00:48:43,070
From a calibration perspective, typically, what is the proportion of your training budget or your training resource budget that goes into calibration versus your general train?

538
00:48:43,080 --> 00:48:45,550
Good question.

539
00:48:45,550 --> 00:48:51,350
I would say a large, large majority of resources goes into training.

540
00:48:51,350 --> 00:48:54,070
So, kind of calibration comes in some sense for free.

541
00:48:54,070 --> 00:48:58,710
It's built into the training process itself through the usage of proper loss functions.

542
00:48:58,710 --> 00:49:08,270
And also, I think what the point is, these post-training phases in LLMs, those are the ones that kind of lobotomize the model.

543
00:49:08,270 --> 00:49:11,911
Here, we don't have to post-train and say, "Oh, does the human prefer this or not?" and things like that.

544
00:49:11,911 --> 00:49:19,070
So, that kind of starts skewing the confidence of LLMs, but here, we don't have to do that.

545
00:49:19,070 --> 00:49:26,950
The quality of the output can be objectively measured, and because of that, the model gets much more kind of reliable, consistent signal during train.

546
00:49:26,960 --> 00:49:30,670
What about in-context training during a session?

547
00:49:30,670 --> 00:49:34,670
Does calibration, because you don't have to a post-training kind of phase, right?

548
00:49:34,670 --> 00:49:41,630
Could it mean that when you're live in context, the predictions can somehow be fed back in that session or won't be equal in there?

549
00:49:41,640 --> 00:49:52,390
Yeah, so the way in-context learning would work in this case is that you would give the model a set of kind of historical examples, and then you would ask it about something that you don't know.

550
00:49:52,390 --> 00:50:00,670
And now, the model gets those historical in context examples that are of course use case, let's say customer database specific, and then you give it something that you don't know about.

551
00:50:00,670 --> 00:50:08,270
You ask it to make a prediction, and this pre-trained model is basically reasoning over those historical examples that you gave it to make you that prediction.

552
00:50:08,270 --> 00:50:11,990
And what is interesting here because if I give you the historical examples,

553
00:50:11,990 --> 00:50:22,070
the model can take a couple of those away and pretend as if it doesn't know it doesn't know the outcome in the past, make a prediction there, and it sees how accurate it is.

554
00:50:22,080 --> 00:50:28,990
And that gives you objective non-hallucinated way to say this is how accurate I am for this prediction, right?

555
00:50:28,990 --> 00:50:33,670
It cannot be overconfident because it can measure itself, let's say, on the historic data.

556
00:50:33,680 --> 00:50:38,870
Now, you also mentioned that this explanation, I guess, comes with a score of confidence, right?

557
00:50:38,880 --> 00:50:43,390
And typically, is it a set of scores, a set of likelihoods?

558
00:50:43,390 --> 00:50:45,030
Like is it per prediction?

559
00:50:45,030 --> 00:50:48,950
I I guess how does a user of this prediction absorb that and, you know, practically use it?

560
00:50:48,960 --> 00:50:51,510
Yeah, that's a great point. So, you would get two things, right?

561
00:50:51,510 --> 00:50:53,790
Or three things.

562
00:50:53,790 --> 00:51:04,630
You get a prediction, you get a confidence interval accuracy estimate, and then you also get the explanation in natural language about why did the model make that prediction.

563
00:51:04,630 --> 00:51:06,430
And the way we do this, it's actually super cool.

564
00:51:06,440 --> 00:51:12,710
It's allows us to basically back trace the model and see what data is it attending over.

565
00:51:12,720 --> 00:51:14,310
All right?

566
00:51:14,310 --> 00:51:22,910
And then we can basically look at that attention mechanism, the tables, the columns, the rows that the model is attending over, and then synthesize that into an explanation.

567
00:51:22,910 --> 00:51:29,230
And we can say prediction for this is like that because of the data in this table, the data in that table, and so on.

568
00:51:29,240 --> 00:51:42,390
That's what I would say is the exciting part is that you can truly make these explanations not just by the signals or features that somebody sought to engineer, but actually directly from the raw data.

569
00:51:42,390 --> 00:51:45,910
So, the fidelity or richness of these explanations is actually quite impressive.

570
00:51:45,910 --> 00:51:49,710
You kind of learn something new because it's hidden in the data and you didn't actually know about it.

571
00:51:49,710 --> 00:51:51,630
You didn't have to pre-engineer it.

572
00:51:51,640 --> 00:51:54,070
Interesting. So, it's almost like a debugging trace.

573
00:51:54,080 --> 00:51:56,390
Great point.

574
00:51:56,390 --> 00:52:04,230
It's also we see many times it's a debugging trace, because what you can easily detect with this is to say, "Oh, actually I have some information leakage.

575
00:52:04,230 --> 00:52:06,030
I have some data in there that I shouldn't have in there."

576
00:52:06,030 --> 00:52:08,950
And things like that. Exactly. So, you can think of it as a debugging trace.

577
00:52:08,960 --> 00:52:15,950
But instead of trace hitting endpoints or methods or function calls, you're actually talking about which piece of data in your database you're asking.

578
00:52:15,960 --> 00:52:18,310
What was the weight? What was the process?

579
00:52:18,320 --> 00:52:19,830
Exactly.

580
00:52:19,830 --> 00:52:21,550
It's about almost like debugging the data in a sense, right?

581
00:52:21,560 --> 00:52:27,030
Especially when things go wrong, it's a super elegant way to debug what's happening.

582
00:52:27,030 --> 00:52:30,110
Or when a prediction is made, you can use it for justification and things like that.

583
00:52:30,120 --> 00:52:32,870
So, as you debug it, how would you use this to effectively debug it?

584
00:52:32,870 --> 00:52:34,990
Let's say I found a trace.

585
00:52:34,990 --> 00:52:39,510
I've noticed that again, this is just visually for my own modeling, right?

586
00:52:39,510 --> 00:52:44,510
You find that in this query, the prediction used table X and not Y.

587
00:52:44,510 --> 00:52:47,311
Or it used relationship A and not B. Yeah.

588
00:52:47,311 --> 00:52:51,031
I'm I'm assuming it looks like that, but maybe not. But if you had that, what would you do with it? And who would do it?

589
00:52:51,040 --> 00:52:54,550
That's great, right? So, there are several different ways.

590
00:52:54,550 --> 00:53:04,550
If you are the one who's developing the model, what you can learn from this is that maybe you have a table in there that is leaking information.

591
00:53:04,550 --> 00:53:11,190
Or maybe you learn that some data is important for prediction, but actually that data is not available at the time of prediction.

592
00:53:11,190 --> 00:53:12,750
But the model is going to use it.

593
00:53:12,750 --> 00:53:15,310
So, you can kind of take that data away.

594
00:53:15,310 --> 00:53:24,230
Another way that can happen is that if you see that model is attending over particular table of over a particular type of data, that also tells you, "Oh, there is signal in here."

595
00:53:24,230 --> 00:53:27,790
You can bring additional data, link in another tables, and this way improve the accuracy of the model.

596
00:53:27,790 --> 00:53:36,990
So, it's both good for improving the model, as well as kind of finding, I would say, bugs in the data,

597
00:53:36,990 --> 00:53:42,750
having columns that maybe, you know, somehow were in there, but shouldn't be there because they are backfilled and things like that.

598
00:53:42,750 --> 00:53:54,670
So, of these use cases would be solved by this by providing ideas on what you can do to improve as well as kind of debugging the data where the model is shockingly good and you're like, "Hey, something seems fishy here."

599
00:53:54,680 --> 00:53:57,510
Yeah, it's almost like a really good forensic investigator tool as well.

600
00:53:57,510 --> 00:54:00,870
You know, you you don't know what you don't know and you're finding out things that you didn't know.

601
00:54:00,880 --> 00:54:02,430
Exactly.

602
00:54:02,430 --> 00:54:11,190
You can think of it as a forensic tool and you can also where it becomes very useful is because you can start asking these models what is called technically counterfactual questions.

603
00:54:11,200 --> 00:54:16,550
You can say, "If I do this, what would happen?

604
00:54:16,550 --> 00:54:18,590
If I send this offer to the customer, what happens to the customer?"

605
00:54:18,600 --> 00:54:20,270
Right?

606
00:54:20,270 --> 00:54:29,790
So, now you can kind of test all these alternative hypothesis and ask the model, "Okay, like we had a use case for sales lead scoring and then the person who was using the system was like,

607
00:54:29,790 --> 00:54:34,590
"Hey, why did you predict so low probability of me closing this deal?"

608
00:54:34,590 --> 00:54:38,590
And you can go back to the model and the model is like, "Look, I'm looking at the data.

609
00:54:38,590 --> 00:54:42,990
Your deal size is very large compared to other deals.

610
00:54:42,990 --> 00:54:45,710
I'm looking here, you don't have an executive sponsor.

611
00:54:45,710 --> 00:54:49,630
And also, by the way, this data hasn't been updated for 3 months."

612
00:54:49,640 --> 00:54:55,191
Now, the salesperson can be like, "Ooh, okay, actually, there is new information. We forgot to update.

613
00:54:55,191 --> 00:54:57,830
Let's put that data in."

614
00:54:57,830 --> 00:54:59,950
And you can query the model again.

615
00:54:59,950 --> 00:55:03,711
Or you can start asking, "Okay, I don't have an executive sponsor. Let me figure that out."

616
00:55:03,711 --> 00:55:14,110
And then, you can be also asking this counterfactual question, which is like, "Okay, so now if I offer the client 10% discount, what happens to the probability of closing?"

617
00:55:14,110 --> 00:55:17,390
This is kind of an example Yeah, how this capability could be used.

618
00:55:17,400 --> 00:55:31,790
That's a really good example, actually. So, how much of this, I'll use the word fine-tuning loosely, how much of this adding more data, doing more fine-tuning or an RFM can you do before you might say you hit the limit and you have to retrain or go with a newer model?

619
00:55:31,790 --> 00:55:37,070
Is there kind of any rough rule of thumb there or is it just one day we'll get there?

620
00:55:37,080 --> 00:55:46,630
I would say generally more data that is actually useful and relevant allows you to train more accurate models.

621
00:55:46,630 --> 00:55:52,350
So, we want to use the data that is relevant and as much of it as possible.

622
00:55:52,350 --> 00:55:55,870
But also these methods, you know, are not that data hungry.

623
00:55:55,870 --> 00:56:00,830
We have use cases where maybe we have an order of thousands of examples.

624
00:56:00,830 --> 00:56:04,830
And we have use cases where we have an order of tens of billions of use cases.

625
00:56:04,830 --> 00:56:10,990
And the beautiful thing is that you can basically then choose your model size, architecture, that kind of fits your needs.

626
00:56:11,000 --> 00:56:12,790
Okay, so that sounds good.

627
00:56:12,790 --> 00:56:16,190
One big hot topic is agentic systems, right?

628
00:56:16,190 --> 00:56:21,870
How do you see this kind of interdependence, symbiosis between RFMs and agentic systems?

629
00:56:21,880 --> 00:56:30,430
Yeah, we see a lot of traction there because, you know, for agents, specially in enterprise business settings, they need to be able to make decisions.

630
00:56:30,430 --> 00:56:35,030
All right, and even if you say, "I am a customer support agent."

631
00:56:35,030 --> 00:56:42,110
That customer support agent actually has a lot of predictive decision-making tasks to make before it reacts.

632
00:56:42,110 --> 00:56:45,390
Right, it's about what's the lifetime value of this customer?

633
00:56:45,390 --> 00:56:48,430
How likely is this customer going to churn?

634
00:56:48,430 --> 00:56:52,870
What is the next best action that I take to keep this customer?

635
00:56:52,870 --> 00:56:58,951
What is the offer or resolution I should offer to increase the probability of customer not churning and so on. Right?

636
00:56:58,951 --> 00:57:08,350
And today, I think we are early in agents, so we get excited about this almost like this kind of, "Oh, let's retrieve some knowledge from some knowledge base and reformat it and things like that."

637
00:57:08,350 --> 00:57:12,830
But as these agents become more business critical, more autonomous,

638
00:57:12,830 --> 00:57:23,471
they need this decision-making power and reasoning ability over the enterprise structured operational data to make the correct decisions. Right?

639
00:57:23,471 --> 00:57:29,110
And if you now start thinking about a customer agent, one of course important thing is to talk, to understand, and things like that.

640
00:57:29,110 --> 00:57:41,070
But at the same time, it's like having the right tone, giving the right offer, and making sure that, you know, customers are satisfied, that directly affects the effectiveness. So, we see a lot of need for that.

641
00:57:41,080 --> 00:57:49,710
Could you share examples of where a customer has switched their agent to use an RFM versus a classical LLM, and how it improved the outcome?

642
00:57:49,720 --> 00:57:52,150
A great example is also in sales, right?

643
00:57:52,160 --> 00:58:01,510
If you say about, let's understand the probability of closing, uh let's understand what's the next best action to take here,

644
00:58:01,510 --> 00:58:13,230
let's understand what's the next product to upsell to that client so that they increase their spend, let's understand what me as a salesperson should do next to be more effective.

645
00:58:13,230 --> 00:58:16,910
We see great results in these types of domains.

646
00:58:16,910 --> 00:58:22,190
Uh LLMs cannot make, I mean, they make this kind of good human-level common-sense decisions,

647
00:58:22,190 --> 00:58:31,110
but these dedicated predictive models who have looked through all the patterns of the past are just so much more accurate, right?

648
00:58:31,110 --> 00:58:35,630
They're like 20-30% more accurate than this kind of common-sensey LLM or human can do.

649
00:58:35,630 --> 00:58:47,230
And we've done, like, for example, with some sales teams, we ran AB tests where one part of the sales team would act based on these predictions, and the other one would use LLMs and existing tools, and it was a huge difference.

650
00:58:47,230 --> 00:58:51,390
It was like 30% difference in effectiveness of those teams.

651
00:58:51,390 --> 00:58:57,710
Because they had this structured data understanding, good quantitative predictions, counterfactuals, and things like that.

652
00:58:57,720 --> 00:59:06,190
As you wrap up, I want to get some practical guidance for software engineers or data scientists who are kind of in this space and want to explore this.

653
00:59:06,190 --> 00:59:09,270
What would be the easiest way to start exploring it? I mean, what is the hello world of this?

654
00:59:09,280 --> 00:59:18,510
Yeah, that's a great point. I would say there is a couple of hello worlds, and it depends maybe on the flavor or role, technical sophistication, or I don't know, of people.

655
00:59:18,510 --> 00:59:23,630
I think for researchers, PyG, PyTorch Geometric, is a great starting point.

656
00:59:23,630 --> 00:59:25,470
A real bench and all this stuff.

657
00:59:25,470 --> 00:59:37,910
For people who just want to use this, I think these tabular foundation models, relational foundation models are great and that is both open source as well as basically public as the case when people can start playing with this.

658
00:59:37,910 --> 00:59:40,590
The one I'd recommend is called Kumo RFM.

659
00:59:40,590 --> 00:59:47,190
So, if you go to k u m o r f m like relational foundation model.ai, want to start playing with this.

660
00:59:47,190 --> 00:59:56,510
And this technology is also going to be available soon in large data warehouses like Snowflake and so on so that it's kind of ready to use, pre-installed. One doesn't have to worry too much.

661
